tricter than the decoder, and pinning it would need a ceiling-sized round trip I judged too heavy.\n\nGates at this head, run by me: gofmt, git diff --check, vet, GOOS=windows build, and full go test ./... -count=1 all clean, thirty packages, zero FAIL lines. Seven mutations applied and killed.","body":{"branch":"request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound","conditions":"Review exact clean pushed head fad32567b74250be2efbfb05c0463789b4203cfd on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, reporting artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d60858cd5f0fbf6dfc133832bed0a7ece205c23a at internal/kernel, against parent request bc311b36, your child requests 80320929 and 1c0efc81, and your ratified verdict 152f563c. Record all three conclusions. Architecture: whether the kernel contract, compact checkpoint v3, streamed reads and the documented 256 MiB serialized ceiling hold. Security: whether the sorting scratch is now reserved on both construction encoding paths and released on every exit, whether one shared ceiling makes an undecodable checkpoint unemittable, and whether the allocation sweep in the artifact is complete and truthful about what is charged, estimated and excluded. Simplification: whether the scratch helper, the shared constant, or any test can be cut. Check the artifact text against the code as well as the code itself. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifact. Any change to the head invalidates the verdict.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}